{
  "id": "Compound::DB00564 - Gene::1559",
  "verbalization": "Carbamazepine is known to bind to Cyp2c8 and Cyp3a4, which are both members of the CYP2C family of enzymes. These enzymes are involved in the metabolism of various substrates, including xenobiotics and endogenous compounds. The observed evidence suggests that Carbamazepine may inhibit Cyp2c9 through a mechanism involving its interaction with Cyp2c8, which in turn participates in the oxidation of arachidonic acid. This pathway is consistent with the role of CYP2C enzymes in the metabolism of lipid-derived compounds and their influence on drug response. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential mechanism by which Carbamazepine could modulate Cyp2c9 activity, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence supports a direct relationship between Carbamazepine and Cyp2c9, mediated through the CYP2C family, and underscores the importance of enzyme–substrate interactions in drug metabolism.",
